1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,634
Welcome to lesson twenty: observability.

2
00:00:03,734 --> 00:00:15,696
You'll learn to know what's happening inside your cluster and apps before users tell you: collecting metrics, logs and traces, building useful dashboards, and writing alerts that wake people only for real problems.

3
00:00:17,046 --> 00:00:23,984
Monitoring watches known things, like CPU. Observability lets you answer new questions you didn't plan for.

4
00:00:24,084 --> 00:00:33,534
Metrics tell you how much and how fast. Logs tell you exactly what happened. Traces show where the time went across services. Kubernetes events show what the cluster did.

5
00:00:33,634 --> 00:00:40,908
The best setups connect them, so you can go from an alert to a dashboard, to a slow trace, to the logs of that exact request.

6
00:00:42,258 --> 00:00:47,980
What should you measure? The four golden signals: latency, traffic, errors and saturation.

7
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:57,706
For services, use RED: rate, errors and duration. For resources like nodes and disks, use USE: utilization, saturation and errors.

8
00:00:57,806 --> 00:01:07,064
Then set objectives. An S L I is a measurement, an S L O is the target, and the error budget is how much failure is left. Good alerts are based on these.

9
00:01:08,414 --> 00:01:10,568
Metrics come from several places.

10
00:01:10,668 --> 00:01:15,894
metrics-server only serves autoscaling. It has no history, so it isn't for monitoring.

11
00:01:15,994 --> 00:01:26,404
cAdvisor in the kubelet measures containers. kube-state-metrics reports the state of objects. node-exporter measures nodes. And your apps expose their own business and request metrics.

12
00:01:27,754 --> 00:01:39,476
Prometheus is the standard metrics system for Kubernetes. It pulls metrics from each target's metrics page, stores them as time series, evaluates rules, and sends alerts to Alertmanager.

13
00:01:39,576 --> 00:01:42,834
Each time series is a metric name plus labels.

14
00:01:42,934 --> 00:01:50,848
Counters only go up, so always use rate. Gauges go up and down. And histograms let you calculate percentiles across Pods.

15
00:01:52,198 --> 00:02:00,416
Here are the essential queries: requests per second, the error percentage, and the ninety-fifth percentile latency from a histogram.

16
00:02:00,516 --> 00:02:08,766
Rules of thumb: use a range at least four times the scrape interval, always aggregate after rate, and keep the le label for percentiles.

17
00:02:10,116 --> 00:02:13,998
Every unique combination of label values is a separate time series.

18
00:02:14,098 --> 00:02:24,460
Labels with unbounded values, like user IDs, email addresses, or full URLs with IDs, create millions of series and crash Prometheus.

19
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:29,786
Use route templates instead, and put high-cardinality details in logs and traces.

20
00:02:31,136 --> 00:02:40,986
The usual way to install everything is the kube-prometheus-stack Helm chart. It includes an operator, Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana, and ready-made alerts.

21
00:02:41,086 --> 00:02:50,280
You tell Prometheus what to scrape with a ServiceMonitor. The two most common mistakes are a missing release label, and a port given as a number instead of the Service's port name.

22
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:56,102
For long retention and many clusters, add Thanos or Mimir, or use a managed service.

23
00:02:57,452 --> 00:03:02,934
Here's an alert that fires when the checkout error rate stays above two percent for five minutes.

24
00:03:03,034 --> 00:03:12,676
Alert on symptoms users feel, like errors and latency, not on every cause like high CPU. Every paging alert should be actionable and link to a runbook.

25
00:03:12,776 --> 00:03:20,482
Alertmanager routes, groups and silences alerts. And a watchdog alert that always fires proves your alerting pipeline itself is working.

26
00:03:21,832 --> 00:03:29,874
Apps should log to standard output. The container runtime writes those logs to files on the node, and kubectl logs reads them.

27
00:03:29,974 --> 00:03:39,152
So when a Pod or node goes away, its logs are lost. You need central collection: an agent on every node ships logs to a backend like Loki or OpenSearch.

28
00:03:39,252 --> 00:03:48,190
Use structured JSON logs with a trace ID, never log secrets, and control the volume. Grafana's older Promtail agent has reached end of life.

29
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:57,662
A trace follows one request across services, as a set of timed spans. Here it shows the database query in the payment service is the problem.

30
00:03:57,762 --> 00:04:02,220
Each service must forward the trace context header, or the trace breaks in two.

31
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:11,946
OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral standard for producing and moving traces, metrics and logs. Its Collector receives, processes and exports them to any backend.

32
00:04:13,296 --> 00:04:16,746
In the lab, you'll install the monitoring stack and query it.

33
00:04:16,846 --> 00:04:25,912
You'll likely find some control-plane targets down, because kubeadm binds them to localhost. Many teams never notice they have no control-plane monitoring.

34
00:04:26,012 --> 00:04:33,750
You'll scrape an app, calculate its RED metrics, watch an alert fire and resolve, and see logs disappear with a deleted Pod.

35
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:36,278
Let's recap.

36
00:04:36,378 --> 00:04:40,740
Collect metrics, logs, traces and events, and connect them.

37
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:47,122
Measure golden signals, use rate on counters and histograms for percentiles, and keep labels bounded.

38
00:04:47,222 --> 00:04:53,376
Alert on symptoms with runbooks, collect logs centrally, and use OpenTelemetry for traces.

39
00:04:53,476 --> 00:04:57,934
Next, you'll harden the cluster and secure your software supply chain.
